Fetal alcohol spectrum disorder
Disabilities & Congenital Disorders
Smooth philtrum/thin upper lip, Short palpebral fissures, Growth retardation, Executive function deficits

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: DCD-020
Reason for Visit: Acute behavioral decompensation, severe impulsivity, and risk of elopement.
Location: Private residence (Foster care)
Present at Visit: Patient and foster mother
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported an 8-year-old child with FASD experiencing a severe, prolonged meltdown characterized by extreme hyperactivity, aggression towards the foster mother, and multiple attempts to run onto a busy road over the past 3 hours. Assessment could not be delayed until the next in-hours period due to the imminent risk of serious accidental trauma (elopement) and the need for acute pharmacological de-escalation in a child with profound executive function deficits.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: He tore his room apart, hit me with a chair, and keeps trying to unlock the front door to run into the street.
History of Present Illness: The foster mother reports a 3-hour history of severe behavioral dysregulation. The patient has a documented history of maternal alcohol consumption during pregnancy and exhibits prominent executive function deficits, hyperactivity, and poor impulse control. The current episode was triggered by a minor change in the evening routine (dinner was served 15 minutes late). Since then, the patient has demonstrated extreme hyperactivity, significant global developmental delay in emotional regulation, and impaired fine motor skills resulting in chaotic, destructive behavior. The child is currently screaming, unable to be redirected, and actively attempting to abscond.
Functional Status: Ambulant but entirely dependent on the caregiver for safety and emotional regulation.
Social/Safety Concerns: Immediate risk of death or serious injury due to elopement into traffic; caregiver is physically exhausted.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 110/70 mmHg | HR: 120 bpm | Temp: 36.9 C | RR: 26 bpm | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 21.0 Kg (Low for age)
General: Child is intensely agitated, pacing rapidly, and exhibiting loud, disorganized speech. Characteristic facial features are noted (smooth philtrum, thin vermilion border, short palpebral fissures).
Physical Exam: Physical exam reveals a child with pre- and post-natal growth retardation resulting in short stature and low body weight. Microcephaly is evident. Neurological exam reveals profound distractibility and inability to follow single-step commands due to severe inattention. No focal motor deficits, though fine motor skills are visibly clumsy during the agitation. No signs of acute physical illness (no fever, clear chest, soft abdomen).
Environmental Observations: Front door is barricaded with a heavy couch; the living room is heavily disrupted with thrown objects.
Medication Reconciliation: Routine daily Guanfacine administered this morning; PRN Melatonin available but ineffective for acute daytime agitation.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Fetal alcohol spectrum disorder (with acute severe behavioral dysregulation and elopement risk) - Status: Acute complication
Differentials:

Acute Head Injury - Ruled out/unlikely as the cause of the agitation, given the agitation began purely as a response to a routine change without a preceding fall or trauma, and pupils are equal/reactive.

Undiagnosed Pain (e.g., Dental or ear infection) - Ruled out/unlikely due to the absence of fever, no localized pulling at the ears/jaw, and the clear temporal link to an environmental trigger (routine disruption).

Oppositional Defiant Disorder (Primary) - Ruled out/unlikely as the sole driver, because the patient's organic brain damage (FASD) causes a genuine neurological inability to regulate impulses (executive dysfunction), rather than calculated defiance.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient's acute presentation of extreme hyperactivity, aggression, and relentless elopement attempts represents a crisis of executive dysfunction. The objective findings of tachycardia, disorganized behavior, and the need to barricade the door confirmed that the environment was critically unsafe. This assessment could not be safely delayed until the next in-hours period because the child's severe impulsivity and lack of safety awareness created an imminent risk of fatal trauma (running into traffic), necessitating urgent, in-home pharmacological calming and environmental securing.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered a stat oral dose of Promethazine to provide acute sedation and break the hyperactive cycle. Advised the foster mother on creating a highly structured, low-stimulation environment for the next 12 hours.
Positive Behavioral Change: When you use a visual timer for any routine changes, he can "see" the time passing; therefore, setting this timer 10 minutes before a transition gives his brain the necessary processing time to prevent a meltdown. When you physically lock the deadbolt and keep the key on your person, the elopement pathway is definitively blocked; making this a permanent habit removes the primary source of panic during an escalation.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Urgent referral to the complex behavior support team for a review of his daily medication and crisis plan.
DME/Safety (Optional): Recommended the installation of high-placed slide locks or a monitored alarm system on all exterior doors.
Follow-up: GP review tomorrow to assess the effect of sedation and review ongoing management strategies.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Call 000 for police and ambulance support if he manages to unlock the door and abscond, or if he becomes a physical danger to you that you cannot manage even after the medication.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The sedation is a temporary measure to ensure immediate safety; it does not treat the underlying neurological deficits of FASD.
Clinical Course: The promethazine will induce drowsiness within 45 minutes, allowing his nervous system to reset. The underlying executive dysfunction is a lifelong challenge requiring intensive behavioral supports.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Monitor him closely as he becomes drowsy. Ensure he is sleeping in a safe position. Seek urgent medical care if he becomes completely unarousable or if his breathing becomes very slow and shallow.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Because of his FASD, his brain's "brakes" do not work properly, so when he gets upset, he cannot stop himself. The medication acts as an artificial brake to calm his nervous system down so he can rest safely.
Actionable Steps: Keep the front door key on your person at all times. Use visual timers for all transitions. Install high locks on the doors tomorrow.

